WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:07.540
SPEAKER: Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.207
Thank you very much for coming.

00:00:09.207 --> 00:00:11.290
This is my first time
speaking at a movie theater.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.230
I think your seats are
fully automated, so

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.796
recline, get comfortable.

00:00:15.796 --> 00:00:17.170
I'd also like to
welcome everyone

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.910
who's listening online.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.060
I'm here to help you transform
your business with Cloud TPUs.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.450
So this talk is
about supercomputers,

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.600
machine learning.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.780
These multiple
generations of custom

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.672
A6 boards, systems, and
whole supercomputers

00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:33.130
that we've designed
here at Google,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.470
and are making available
through the cloud.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.270
I've got lots of
interesting things

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.410
to say about our recent
product announcements,

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.280
some new performance and
cost information, and more.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.870
But before we get
to that, I just

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.570
want to set the context
here for those of you who

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.030
are new to the field, or new
to hardware acceleration,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.900
especially in the cloud.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.140
So people have been calling the
end of Moore's Law for decades

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.760
now, so I'm a little
nervous about this.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.730
But I think it's finally over.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.490
Sequential processing
is finally plateauing,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.730
so you can't just wait
for new processors

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.800
to come along and take
your sequential program

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.170
and make it
magically run faster.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.340
And we're reaching this plateau,
after this amazing wild ride,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.500
just at the moment that
businesses have to manage

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.760
more data than ever before.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.550
Now, you're all familiar with
these different kinds of data.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.600
In the images and
video category,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.280
you might have
billions of images

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.190
of user-generated
content, or live video

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.775
streams for video
calls and short videos

00:01:32.775 --> 00:01:34.400
that people are
sharing with each other

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.150
or sharing to show products or
real estate or other spaces.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.520
You've got product imagery
for retail and e-commerce.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.550
You'll hear a little bit
more about that later.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.740
Do you have spatial imagery,
in ever higher resolution,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.695
medical imagery, navigational
imagery for autonomous

00:01:49.695 --> 00:01:51.510
vehicles, robotics,
manufacturing

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.300
for quality control, and more?

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.456
In the text, speech,
and audio domain,

00:01:56.456 --> 00:01:58.080
there are messaging
systems with people

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.050
sending uncountably
many messages every day.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.970
Product reviews, forum
comments that you need

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.910
to moderate and rank and route.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.940
New voice interfaces that are
taking the world by storm.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.400
Support requests that can
come in many modalities,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.260
whether in-person, texting,
voice calls, even video.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.420
You got lots of call
center audio accumulating,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.550
and you want to get
business insights out of it.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.840
And then music
recommendation is just

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.930
an entire world unto itself.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.540
And these are just a
sample of the types

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.810
of data that's accumulating
across all of your businesses

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.346
that could be very
valuable for you to process

00:02:34.346 --> 00:02:35.970
with this cutting-edge
machine learning

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.920
and these cutting-edge
supercomputers.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.820
So the good news is there's
been tremendous progress working

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.620
with unstructured
data, especially

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.710
over the past few years.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.470
As you can see here, looking
at image recognition,

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.740
in this case on the
ImageNet standard benchmark,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.530
there have been dramatic
increases in the top-1 accuracy

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.887
in just five years.

00:02:55.887 --> 00:02:57.970
And the accuracy continues
to increase, especially

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.386
with some of these
new techniques,

00:02:59.386 --> 00:03:03.110
like Learning to
Learn or Auto Amount.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.520
And it's not just
image recognition.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.910
This is not just about ImageNet.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:10.280
These new deep neural
networks, and a sort

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.620
of broader class of
computationally intensive

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.350
new machine learning algorithms,
provide a unified approach

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.870
across many different
application domains,

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.900
ranging from imagery to
speech to text to scenes.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.770
And this is a huge change from
when I was in grad school,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.750
doing my PhD in computer vision.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:29.570
The techniques weren't
very similar between say,

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.150
computer vision and natural
language processing.

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.604
Whereas now, you can use a
single computational framework,

00:03:35.604 --> 00:03:37.520
whether that's TensorFlow
or another framework

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.720
of your choice, to express
many different model

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.840
architectures that all are built
under the same building blocks.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.589
And those are the kinds
of building blocks

00:03:46.589 --> 00:03:49.930
that we can now accelerate
with this new custom hardware.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:53.080
So how do we manage
these profound increases

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:55.734
in computer requirements for
the most valuable new machine

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:56.650
learning applications?

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:01.660
Well, as you can see here, going
back to the image recognition

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.110
challenge on ImageNet,
the vertical axis

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.632
here is accuracy
of these models,

00:04:07.632 --> 00:04:09.340
sort of a selection
of interesting models

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:10.790
over the past few years.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.850
And then the horizontal
axis is computational cost.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.970
And as you can see, to
get to those highest

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:21.070
levels of accuracy, you tend to
need more and more computation,

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.980
processing each image, and also
to train these models, even

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.620
on this data set of a
fixed size with ImageNet.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.140
And so, in some
applications, you

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.570
can get away with
a lower accuracy.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:32.512
And that's fine.

00:04:32.512 --> 00:04:33.970
But in other
applications, when you

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:37.360
think about autonomous driving
or medical imaging or others,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.730
every additional percentage
point of accuracy

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.750
is literally saving lives.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.390
And so there's real urgency
to get at this space that's

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:48.640
currently bounded
by the computation

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.910
we can afford to apply.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.660
So OpenAI recently published a
blog post called AI and Compute

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.250
where they did an analysis
similar to this one.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.420
But now it's on a log scale.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.410
You can see the vertical axis
is computation cost on a log

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.070
scale.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.140
And then here they've
plotted this over time,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.210
and highlighted
several models that

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.290
are especially well-known that
have provided new capabilities

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.780
over the past few years,
starting with AlexNet, which

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.750
kicked off this most
recent wave of excitement

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.160
about machine learning.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.650
And then you see even
better image classification

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.710
models, as I showed
on the previous slide,

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.331
require even more compute.

00:05:24.331 --> 00:05:25.830
But, again, it's
not just restricted

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:26.705
to image recognition.

00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:28.500
We have speech
recognition models

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.270
like DeepSpeech2 in here.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:32.550
We have machine translation
models requiring even more

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.610
compute to deliver these
amazing breakthrough results

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.410
in neural machine translation.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.840
We have real-time game-playing
that OpenAI is working on,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.030
and there will be more
this August there.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:45.780
And then, also, world champion
Go-playing programs, up here,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.590
demanding tremendous
amounts of compute

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:49.680
for the self-play
to learn the game

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.330
and learn the strategies
all on its own.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.290
So if you step back and look at
this picture, what you really

00:05:55.290 --> 00:06:00.125
see is that to get at these
valuable new capabilities,

00:06:00.125 --> 00:06:01.500
you need to throw
as much compute

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:02.880
as you can at the problem.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.920
And that's what's been driving
all this progress over time.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.380
So OpenAI called out a
couple of important insights

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.030
that I'd like to highlight here.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.380
And I won't read all these out.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.530
I recommend that you look at
the blog post in its entirety.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:20.100
But I'd say that, first
of all, the simple rule

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.140
seems to hold across a
wide variety of domains

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.460
that more compute seems
to lead predictably

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.640
to a little better
performance, as they say here

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.820
in the blog post.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.030
And it's complementary
to algorithmic advances.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.829
So it's great to try new
algorithms and new ideas.

00:06:33.829 --> 00:06:36.120
And as I'll say later, there
are still lots of progress

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.620
to be made that way.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.170
But that's complementary
to also having

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.420
an enormous amount of
compute to drive accuracy

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.550
as far as you can go
with the best ideas

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.260
that you've uncovered.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.970
And now, in the lower
right here, this

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.570
is the most interesting insight
that the number to track

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.300
is not the speed of
your single accelerator

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.190
or the capacity of your
biggest data center,

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.950
but it's something in between.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.120
It's the amount of compute
that you can productively

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.684
apply to train a single model.

00:07:02.684 --> 00:07:04.350
This is what the folks
at OpenAI believe

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:06.840
is most likely to correlate
to how powerful the very

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.954
best models in the field are.

00:07:08.954 --> 00:07:11.370
And it's really this that's
driving us-- not just to build

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.400
new chips or new
single machine systems,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.710
but these connected
supercomputers that we call TPU

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:20.370
pods, because those are
enabling us to apply the largest

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:24.060
possible amount of compute
to train a single model that

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.610
can also be subdivided, too,
to train many models at once.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:31.570
So how does this overview
relate to your business?

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.600
Well, there are two
perspectives that I

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.335
think you might find valuable.

00:07:35.335 --> 00:07:37.710
You can come at this from the
point of view of the people

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.168
that you have in
your organization,

00:07:39.168 --> 00:07:41.310
or that you're hoping to
hire, or acquire and bring

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:42.182
in their companies.

00:07:42.182 --> 00:07:44.640
You can also think about this
from the perspective of data.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.200
And I'll go through
these in turn.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.240
So, first of all, people.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.120
If you look around
in your organization,

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.610
and you have people who call
themselves machine learning

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:56.684
engineers, or machine
learning researchers,

00:07:56.684 --> 00:07:59.100
it's obvious that they need
an enormous amount of compute.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.260
They're typically
just budget-limited.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.330
No matter how much
compute you have,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.060
with all these models
that are so compute-hungry

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:07.800
and these enormous
data sets, they'll

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.630
rapidly find a way
to use the compute

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:10.680
that they have available.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:11.970
And they'll ask you for more.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:15.000
You're probably already hearing
them ask for as much compute

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.770
as they can get.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.630
Also data scientists, though.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:20.490
We and other teams
across the industry

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.950
are working hard to make
it easier and easier

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.520
to apply these techniques.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:26.730
So even if you're
not ready to write

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.750
a model in raw
TensorFlow, that's

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.490
OK, because there are
more and more open

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:33.659
source reference
models available that

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:36.330
deliver high performance,
and can easily be adapted,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.240
and can fit into sort of a more
standard data science workflow,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.179
too, even for folks who don't
have a PhD in machine learning.

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:44.640
That's not required.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.310
Furthermore, over in
product development,

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.670
it's an open question how to
take all these new capabilities

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.520
that are just newly being
exposed with machine learning

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.250
and use them to unlock new kinds
of experiences and services.

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:59.550
I think we're just in
the early stages of that.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.425
At Google I/O this
year, we showed a demo

00:09:01.425 --> 00:09:04.380
of Google Duplex, which is
a new way of interacting

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.180
with businesses
through the assistant.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.160
That's just one example
of what I believe

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.210
is going to be a whole
forest of new opportunities

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.550
for new products to
take these capabilities

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:18.221
and integrate them in a
natural way into our lives.

00:09:18.221 --> 00:09:20.720
Once you have those products,
and they're out in production,

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.180
your computer needs to scale
with your number of users.

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:24.920
If you have something
that hosted server-side,

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:26.420
and you have a
billion users, you've

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.310
got to handle billions
of requests coming in

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:31.730
simultaneously, and that
requires a lot of compute.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:34.880
And in between,
research to production,

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.260
and, you know, even
once a model is live,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.770
it's really important to
do continuous integration

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:40.877
and testing.

00:09:40.877 --> 00:09:43.460
Machine learning models look a
lot different from the software

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:45.540
that you're probably
used to testing.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.870
Subtle changes in
your input data

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.090
or any layer of
your compute stack

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.250
all the way down
can lead to changes

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:53.510
in accuracy of your model.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:54.860
And so, it's important.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.990
And what we do at
Google is we're

00:09:56.990 --> 00:10:00.110
retraining these models all
the time on every new version

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:02.540
of our software, and
testing for regressions,

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:04.940
and passing as
much of that as we

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.382
can along to you through our
open source reference models.

00:10:07.382 --> 00:10:08.840
And I'll say more
about that later.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.881
But you'll need to do
this, too, for the products

00:10:10.881 --> 00:10:13.680
that you develop and launch.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:15.810
And I listed enthusiastic
interns here,

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.870
because I think it's
important not just to make

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:22.340
the compute available to
the people with the most

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:27.770
seniority, or the most obvious
titles in your organization,

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:29.570
like the ones that
I've listed before.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:31.235
This whole field
is moving so fast.

00:10:31.235 --> 00:10:33.110
There are so many students
being trained both

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.270
formally and informally
that it's possible

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:38.000
that someone who's just joined
your company as an intern,

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:39.950
who's sufficiently
motivated, could deliver

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.260
an enormous breakthrough
if they have access

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:45.410
to this level of compute,
and can work with the data

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:47.760
that you have, and talk
to your product team.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.640
So I think it's really
important to make

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:52.940
this new large-scale
compute widely available,

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.620
both inside your
organizations and outside.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:59.130
And that's what we're trying
to help all of you do.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:02.750
So, in the past, the key
determinant of progress

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:06.110
was typically waiting for
your code to compile, right?

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.450
There's that famous
XKCD cartoon about this.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.690
Now, in this new world of
working with enormous data sets

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.820
and training these gigantic
accurate ML models,

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.830
and then serving them,
the key determinant

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.630
of progress of your
team is how long

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:21.170
it takes for them to wait for
the machine learning models

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:22.490
to train.

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.470
And for production
models, this can currently

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.520
take weeks or even months.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.840
And so, imagine if your
code took months to compile.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.680
It's really important to pull
that in to hours, minutes, even

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.930
seconds, to make your
teams more productive,

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:38.060
let them try multiple
things at once,

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.970
get results, and iterate
instead of just waiting

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:43.980
for days or weeks or longer.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.850
So now looking at this
from the data perspective.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.880
Now, the kinds of ideal datasets
that I described earlier

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.610
for cutting-edge ML typically
have hundreds of thousands

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.500
of data points,
millions of data points,

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:56.910
or even billions of
data points in them.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:12:00.110
And in most, but not all, of
these cutting-edge domains,

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:02.300
it's important to have
high-quality labels,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.430
or at least the
ability to create them.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:09.470
So typically with user-generated
content, photos, videos,

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:12.560
you might have tags that your
users are already applying.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.230
In other cases, when you're
collecting geospatial imagery,

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:16.940
you can build a team
to label it or do

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:20.050
some sort of augmented
approach to labeling it.

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:23.020
And there are many labeling
services coming online now.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.400
But there are also all
these sensors going out,

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.650
whether it's in mobile phones,
or autonomous vehicles,

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:30.560
or new kinds of robots, or
factories, or elsewhere.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.540
And those are all
streaming data in that

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:35.600
can be valuable for
cutting-edge machine learning.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:38.240
Simulations, too,
can be a valuable way

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.370
to generate data, whether
game environments,

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:43.700
or robotics simulations, or
other types of simulators.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:48.110
And I call out here explicitly
at the bottom agents and robots

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:49.444
interacting with an environment.

00:12:49.444 --> 00:12:51.776
You're currently seeing this
mostly on the research side

00:12:51.776 --> 00:12:52.310
now.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.684
But I think it won't be long
before you start seeing this

00:12:54.684 --> 00:12:55.880
in real production systems.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.550
But these are just
starting points for you

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:00.470
to look around your
business at all the data

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:02.750
that you have or could
easily collect and look

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.000
for data that's related
to the prediction problems

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:06.170
that matter.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:06.920
Right?

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.636
And once you find a
large dataset there,

00:13:09.636 --> 00:13:11.510
that's a good starting
point for figuring out

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.180
how to take this technology
and have an immediate impact

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.810
on your business.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.170
So suppose you have the
people, you have the data.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:19.020
What do you do next?

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:22.580
Well, I recommend that you
access the fastest hardware

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:24.750
that you can, and
ideally in the cloud.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.110
And you might say, well,
what's the advantage

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:28.160
of going to the cloud?

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.690
I could just buy
some accelerators

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:30.620
and plug them in here.

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:31.870
We already have a data center.

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:33.860
That's where our data is.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:35.210
Why should I change?

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:38.870
Well, the advantages,
especially of Google Cloud,

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.540
over on-prem hosting of
your own accelerators

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:43.734
are that you get a
lot of flexibility.

00:13:43.734 --> 00:13:45.650
When you're planning for
your own data center,

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:49.640
there are these enormous lead
times, the capital expenses.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:51.740
It's very complicated
to set things up.

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:53.680
And these new accelerators
are power-dense.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.070
They're networking-dense.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:58.190
They change a lot of
things about the way

00:13:58.190 --> 00:13:59.990
that you need to plan
your data center.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:03.170
We announced at Google I/O that
our Version 3 TPUs are actually

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:03.890
liquid cooled.

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:05.899
So, you know, if you
think that that's

00:14:05.899 --> 00:14:07.440
a signal of where
the field is going,

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.170
do you want to bring liquid
cooling into your data center?

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:10.400
It's complicated.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:12.980
Whereas in the cloud, you can
just mix and match in software

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:14.300
the components that you need.

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.210
You can provision quickly and
scale up as your needs change,

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:20.210
or scale back down if a
burst of traffic has passed,

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.910
and now that you're
in a short lull.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:24.230
There's built-in security.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:25.688
I think one thing
that you've heard

00:14:25.688 --> 00:14:28.010
throughout this conference
is that Google Cloud is

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.360
the leader in security.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:31.580
And we have powerful
tools for data access user

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:32.827
control and more.

00:14:32.827 --> 00:14:34.910
I encourage you to consult
the other sessions just

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:37.430
to see how deep
Google's commitment is

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.740
to security in the cloud.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.240
And as I mentioned
at the beginning,

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:43.760
reducing capital expenses
is really fantastic.

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.560
On-demand pricing and
preemptible pricing

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.170
lets you pay only for what you
need, and scale up and down.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:51.450
So now let's talk
about these TPUs.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:55.710
And these TPUs are
only in Google Cloud.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.990
Our Version 1 is actually
not in the cloud.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.240
This is in our data
center since 2015.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:03.930
It was our first foray into the
space, when Jeff Dean worried

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:06.084
that if every Android user
talked to their phones

00:15:06.084 --> 00:15:08.250
for just three minutes a
day, the speech recognition

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:10.110
algorithm that we were
running at the time

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.690
would have required us to double
our number of data centers.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:13.740
So that wasn't going to work.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.000
And so there was
this crash project

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.583
to develop an
accelerator that you now

00:15:16.583 --> 00:15:19.140
interact with every
time you run a search,

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:21.150
or you use Google
Photos, or street view,

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.860
or a bunch of Google's
other services,

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:26.440
are running either on
this or its successors.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.560
Now here is the Cloud TPU.

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:33.100
This is an example of Google's
second generation TPUs

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.770
in a system that's
widely available today.

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:36.670
No signup required.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:37.510
No waiting.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:39.190
This is now generally available.

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:40.650
It's in the United States.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:41.800
It's in Europe.

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:43.180
It's in Asia.

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:48.110
And you can find out more
by going to g.co/cloudtpu.

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:51.140
The nice thing about these
TPUs is that they weren't just

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:52.702
single machine systems, though.

00:15:52.702 --> 00:15:54.410
Like I mentioned
before, they're designed

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:57.410
to be connected together into
these enormous supercomputers

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:59.180
that we call TPU pods.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:00.870
And the exciting
thing here, that's

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:03.410
been mentioned obliquely, but
I want to make very clear,

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:06.950
is that our Cloud TPU
pod with these v2 TPUs

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:08.690
is now available in alpha.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.700
And you'll hear a little
bit more later about

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:12.440
how people have
already been using it

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:15.350
to accelerate their businesses.

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:17.780
Furthermore, as you may
have heard in the keynotes,

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:20.330
v3 is also in alpha.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:23.360
And so, if you're
interested in trying out

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:25.820
the next generation
of performance,

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:28.130
contact Cloud Sales
or Cloud Support

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:31.510
and get access to
these amazing machines.

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:34.889
Also, we revealed that these
v3 TPUs, just like the v2,

00:16:34.889 --> 00:16:36.430
are designed to be
connected together

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.860
to even larger
liquid-cooled supercomputers

00:16:38.860 --> 00:16:40.900
that we revealed at Google I/O.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:42.760
So what I'm trying
to show you here

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.120
is that we're deeply committed
to making relentless progress

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:50.230
in performance, in cost,
in scale, of these kinds

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:53.410
of accelerators for both
inference and training,

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:57.970
and to pass those benefits
along to you, and in businesses,

00:16:57.970 --> 00:17:01.000
in universities, as
individuals, in startups,

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:02.590
all around the world.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:05.109
So as you can see
here, we've gone

00:17:05.109 --> 00:17:07.420
from 92 teraops
and inference only,

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:11.470
to 180 teraflops for
training and inference

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:13.900
with the Cloud TPU v2.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:17.140
And then with v3, we're now
going with a single device,

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:20.991
that you see pictured
here, up to 420 teraflops.

00:17:20.991 --> 00:17:22.240
We've also doubled the memory.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:24.710
And this is really important
if batch sizes have

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.260
been a limitation
for you, or you

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:27.676
want to go to a
larger models that

00:17:27.676 --> 00:17:30.490
are difficult to fit on a single
device that you have today.

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:33.280
Now suddenly your
device has 420 teraflops

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:35.830
and 128 gigabytes of RAM.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:38.200
Extremely high
bandwidth RAM, actually.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:39.670
So that's in alpha.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:41.500
Check it out.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:44.470
Looking at the pods, the
pod that's available today

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:47.680
is 11 and 1/2 petaflops,
4 terabytes of RAM,

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:50.380
and this mesh network
that makes for really

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:53.980
efficient communication without
any code changes on your part.

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:55.990
And then the TPU v3
pod that we announced

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:59.740
that I/O goes above
100 petaflops with 32

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:01.150
terabytes of RAM.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:05.290
So this is not just thinking
a lot more of the same machine

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:07.850
together, it's a whole
new chip architecture

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.910
in addition to this
larger scale system that

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:12.920
really delivered these results.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:17.070
Furthermore, I want to call
out that, as of yesterday,

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:21.350
here at Cloud Next, Cloud GPU
pricing has gotten even better.

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.900
We've got promotional pricing
that we've introduced across

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:27.680
all of our regions that takes
the price for renting a Cloud

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:33.020
TPU in your central, for
example, down to $4.50 an hour.

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:35.889
And the amazing thing is, if
you can tolerate checkpointing

00:18:35.889 --> 00:18:38.180
and occasionally having to
recover your work if it gets

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:39.890
interrupted, which
is an easy thing

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:42.710
to do in machine learning,
you can use preemptible TPUs

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:45.320
for just $1.35 an hour.

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:48.430
This is an unprecedented
step to make machine learning

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:49.970
more affordable for everyone.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.880
And so we hope that this
helps you both get started

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:55.190
with our new platform
comfortably, and for those

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:56.731
of you in these
larger organizations,

00:18:56.731 --> 00:18:58.460
to scale up way
beyond anything you

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:01.210
are able to contemplate now.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:04.270
So what's the user experience
for these Cloud TPUs?

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:07.840
Well, today, the best way
to program these Cloud TPUs

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:09.160
is with TensorFlow.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:15.069
And, in particular, TensorFlow
is a wide and vast open source

00:19:15.069 --> 00:19:16.360
framework for machine learning.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:17.550
Its the most popular
in the world.

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:19.383
On GitHub, it's one of
the top five projects

00:19:19.383 --> 00:19:20.770
on GitHub overall.

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:23.850
And programming TPUs to get
the highest performance,

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.580
you'll typically use a subset
of the TensorFlow APIs,

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:30.830
tf.data to manage your input
processing, layers, estimators,

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:32.590
and then you go through
the XLA compiler,

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:34.530
over there in the lower right.

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:36.780
To give you a sense of
what this looks like--

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:40.530
and by the way, these APIs
are not specific to TPUs.

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:42.940
If you implement
your models this way,

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:45.100
our intention over
time is for it

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:47.380
to require less and
less change for you

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:49.570
to just flip a
configuration flag

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.041
and run your code
on GPU or on CPU.

00:19:52.041 --> 00:19:54.040
So we're really trying
to get to the point where

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:56.829
you can write your machine
learning models once, and then

00:19:56.829 --> 00:19:58.870
transparently take advantage
of whatever hardware

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:01.330
you have available, and
deploy on whatever hardware

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:04.030
you want out at the edge,
whether that's in a phone,

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:09.650
or in a vehicle, or in some
other edge application.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:12.150
So focusing on layers and
estimators for a second,

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:16.250
though, here's some sample code
of a typical computer vision

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:18.867
model, unmodified for TPU.

00:20:18.867 --> 00:20:21.200
And then here's an example
of the types of modifications

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:22.460
you have to make today.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.650
And, again, we're trying
to reduce these over time.

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:26.570
Just a couple of lines
have changed here

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:30.110
around the optimizer
and the estimator.

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:31.970
And so, I want to
emphasize, though,

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:35.960
that you're not just limited
to TensorFlow programming TPUs.

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:37.582
A lot of people
think, oh, well, what

00:20:37.582 --> 00:20:40.040
if I use some other framework
or what if I want flexibility

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:41.990
to try multiple
ways of interacting

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:43.130
with these accelerators?

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:44.510
That's just fine.

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:47.450
And in fact, as of
TensorFlow 1.10,

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:51.170
we announced here at Cloud
Next that we have initial Keras

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:53.450
integration with Cloud TPUs.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:56.060
And this gives you even more
flexibility and convenience.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:57.560
And it gives you
an incremental path

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:00.140
for taking the Keras
code that you have today,

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.500
running it on the TPU, and
then gradually optimizing

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:05.360
the performance maybe
of the input pipeline

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:07.459
or of other parts of
it to get, to unlock

00:21:07.459 --> 00:21:09.500
more and more of the
performance that these Cloud

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.220
TPUs can deliver.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:14.420
So if you're choosing
between one or the other,

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:16.400
the way to think about
this is, on the left,

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:19.020
TPUEstimator gives
you peak performance.

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:19.520
Right?

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.467
It scales to pods with
zero code changes,

00:21:21.467 --> 00:21:23.300
which is truly amazing,
if you've ever tried

00:21:23.300 --> 00:21:24.710
to write a distributed system.

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:27.020
This is a fantastic convenience.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:29.300
It's got some error robustness.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:31.080
You can easily export
models for inference

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:33.350
on CPUs, GPUs, or TPUs.

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:36.080
And then Keras has sort of
a complementary advantage

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.980
with easy development,
non-static shapes, NumPy input,

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:41.780
and then experimental
tf.data integration.

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:44.720
So definitely recommend
trying them both

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:49.040
and seeing which one
better suits your use case.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.510
Cloud TPUs are
integrated tightly

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:55.100
with TensorBoard, which lets you
track what your model is doing.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:58.340
And we have this
fantastic profiler tab

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:00.950
that lets you
capture TPU profiles

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:02.960
and examine them
in enormous detail.

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:06.110
People have given us tremendous
feedback, positive feedback,

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:08.780
on how valuable this is to
understand what their models

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:10.550
are doing on this new hardware.

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:12.920
This is something
that people really

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.330
love that are using TPUs today.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:17.810
For example, we even
have a frame here

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:18.980
that calls out in red--

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:19.820
I don't know if
you can read it--

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.640
but it's telling you that your
program is not input bounded.

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.830
So this is a great
time to optimize

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:27.710
the mapping of your
model onto the hardware.

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:30.350
Whereas in other cases, it
might say, you're input bounded.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:31.741
Don't worry about the model.

00:22:31.741 --> 00:22:33.740
Optimize your input
pipeline, because you're not

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:35.406
able to feed data
fast enough to keep up

00:22:35.406 --> 00:22:37.830
with these accelerators.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:40.080
I'd also like to emphasize
that Cloud TPUs are

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:43.660
different in another way that
gives you more flexibility.

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:45.180
They're network-attached
rather than

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.610
being directly attached
to your virtual machine.

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:50.220
And what that means is, you,
the customer, here on the left,

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:53.130
this particular customer
is here in the room,

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:56.457
is connected to an
arbitrary Compute Engine VM,

00:22:56.457 --> 00:22:57.290
in the center there.

00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:59.400
That can be whatever
you want-- large, small.

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:01.080
And it can actually
be very small,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:02.970
because all the heavy
compute is happening

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:04.469
behind the scenes
on the Cloud TPUs,

00:23:04.469 --> 00:23:06.240
so that can be an
N1 standard, too.

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:09.000
You don't need a
big VM to drive.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:13.350
And then communicating over
network with this Cloud TPU,

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:14.790
ordinary open
source TensorFlow is

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:16.170
able to drive your computation.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:19.060
You know, reading in data
from GCS or elsewhere.

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:20.940
Now we've got Cloud
Bigtable integrations,

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.460
and gRPC integrations, and
other ways of getting data

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:24.750
into these devices.

00:23:24.750 --> 00:23:26.556
But also that Cloud
TPU doesn't have

00:23:26.556 --> 00:23:27.680
to just be a single device.

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:29.550
It be a whole slice of a pod.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.920
And so this extra
network step means

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.080
you don't have to fight
with drivers anymore.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.360
You just use these machine
images that we provide.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:38.149
And you can scale
up and down easily

00:23:38.149 --> 00:23:39.690
from an infrastructure
point of view,

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.120
as well as from a
code point of view.

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:45.630
So let me say a little bit more
about scaling up on these pods.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:49.170
So from a samples-per-second
point of view,

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:51.510
these pods are
designed to scale.

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:54.240
And so you see perfect
linear scaling behavior

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:56.940
in terms of, in this case,
with Resonant 50, a while

00:23:56.940 --> 00:24:00.000
back, how many images
were processed per second

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.190
as we scaled up the
number of devices

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:06.619
connected with our special
super fast network.

00:24:06.619 --> 00:24:08.160
I just want to
emphasize, again, this

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:10.710
means distributed training
of machine learning models

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:12.150
can finally be easy.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:15.050
There are fantastic
contributions outside.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:16.000
There's TensorFlow.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:16.666
There's Horovod.

00:24:16.666 --> 00:24:18.510
There's other
things, which, if you

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:22.110
have a bespoke cluster within
infiniband that can really

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:24.390
scale, and all the
expertise to operate it,

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:26.290
you can achieve
fantastic results.

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:29.190
But this is, I think,
the easiest way

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:33.720
to achieve this level of scale
in any public cloud today.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:35.670
Furthermore, you
might wonder, well,

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:38.011
but if I make the
batch sizes that large,

00:24:38.011 --> 00:24:39.510
is my model still
going to converge?

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:41.139
And the answer is, it depends.

00:24:41.139 --> 00:24:42.930
But the research is
looking very promising.

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:45.270
There is very active
research on this right now.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:47.550
And what you're seeing
here is on the time axis,

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:50.400
that orange dot is a
single device training

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:52.530
a model to a certain accuracy.

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:55.650
And then you can see here, as
we've tuned the hyperparameters

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.669
and gone to a larger and
larger slice of the pod,

00:24:58.669 --> 00:25:00.210
you can still hit
that same accuracy,

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:02.410
but in much less time.

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:06.540
So I'm thrilled that eBay,
who's been using these Cloud TPU

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:09.190
pods, has been able to take
advantage of this benefit

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:11.410
already for their
visual search models

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:14.260
to achieve enormous
speedups on much larger data

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:15.640
sets than ImageNet.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.240
And one of our
colleagues from eBay's

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:19.070
here in the audience today.

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:21.640
So the challenge
is visual search,

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:23.710
which is an enormous part
of the eBay experience.

00:25:23.710 --> 00:25:26.740
Customers all over the world are
listing more than one billion

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:29.770
products, and need to be
able to find what they want,

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:31.180
when they want it.

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:34.080
But even with the training image
set that's a subset of that,

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:37.330
55 million images, training a
model with on-premise hardware

00:25:37.330 --> 00:25:38.950
was taking months.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:41.620
And it was impossible
to iterate and try out

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:45.570
new things as quickly as the
computer vision team wanted to.

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:49.240
So, to scale further, eBay
switched to the Cloud TPU pods

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:51.290
that are now available
to everybody in alpha,

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:54.140
with the goal of delivering
a new image classification

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:56.720
model optimized for
TPUs, and then finding

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:59.060
the right hyperparameter
settings for their extremely

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:02.180
large dataset.

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:06.540
I'm happy to report that the
results have been fantastic.

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:08.600
And the progress is continuing.

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.292
So, in some cases,
it's hard to do

00:26:10.292 --> 00:26:12.500
an apples-to-apples comparison
between something that

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:14.180
was trained for
months and months

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:16.660
with something that's
trained in just a few days.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:18.230
But in some cases,
the speedup was

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:21.320
almost 100x over
the hardware system

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.390
that they'd been
using previously.

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:25.190
And, when you pick
the best model

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:27.920
of the ones that were
tested, the accuracy boost

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:31.437
was 10%, which is enormous
for a production model.

00:26:31.437 --> 00:26:33.020
So what this means
is that eBay is now

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:35.100
closer to continuous
machine learning, training

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:37.100
multiple models each week
to keep product search

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:40.160
results fresh, pulling
in new advances

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:42.590
from cutting-edge research
that's being published

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:44.150
in the open all the time.

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:45.860
We think this is a
really promising step

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:49.580
towards reinventing the way
that eBay's customers visually

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:52.830
shop and experience the site.

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:54.914
So let me say a little bit
more about performance,

00:26:54.914 --> 00:26:56.621
since I know that
that's something that's

00:26:56.621 --> 00:26:58.740
interesting to all of
you who are pushing up

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:02.230
against the limits of whatever
setup you currently have.

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:06.390
Now, it's important to
measure performance carefully.

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:10.090
Often you'll see
isolated statistics out

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:12.280
of context thrown
around on the internet.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:16.030
This many samples per second,
or this many times faster,

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:17.260
or what have you.

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:20.240
It's really ideal-- it's
not always possible,

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:23.650
but it's really ideal to
focus on real-world data,

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:27.820
on time-to-accuracy, and cost in
an apples-to-apples comparison

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.310
across two different systems.

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:33.010
And so when I say real-world
data, what I mean here

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:34.552
is that often you'll
see results that

00:27:34.552 --> 00:27:36.635
are achieved with synthetic
data, which just means

00:27:36.635 --> 00:27:38.320
random numbers fed
through to the device

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:39.445
to see how fast it can run.

00:27:39.445 --> 00:27:41.320
It's like lifting a
car off the pavement,

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:42.736
and then flooring
the accelerator,

00:27:42.736 --> 00:27:44.506
and measuring how
fast the wheels spin.

00:27:44.506 --> 00:27:45.005
It's cool.

00:27:45.005 --> 00:27:46.660
It makes a loud noise.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.900
But what you really care about
is the whole system performance

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:52.450
of a real dataset
flowing into your system

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.530
without any bottlenecks,
and being processed

00:27:54.530 --> 00:27:57.490
to get to the results that
you want, or serve the models

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:59.530
that matter to your customers

00:27:59.530 --> 00:28:01.780
Also, you'll often hear
people report results

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:03.700
without saying anything
about convergence

00:28:03.700 --> 00:28:05.200
to the expected accuracy.

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.570
But, ultimately, that's
what matters to you

00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:08.650
and your business.

00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:10.900
If you're doing a pedestrian
detector for autonomous

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:13.630
driving, it really is
important how accurate

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:16.000
that pedestrian
detector model is.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:18.589
And even a loss of
a percentage point

00:28:18.589 --> 00:28:21.130
is something that you take very
seriously, and want to avoid.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:24.340
So, finally, we're
trying to move away

00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:27.004
from chip-to-chip or
system-to-system comparisons,

00:28:27.004 --> 00:28:29.170
because they're going to
be increasingly meaningless

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:31.600
as there's this Cambrian
explosion of new hardware

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.156
architectures to deal with
the end of Moore's Law.

00:28:34.156 --> 00:28:35.530
And instead, we're
trying to look

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:39.270
at the total cost of one
system versus another

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:42.220
in the public clouds that
are accessible to everybody.

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:44.160
Finally, we're investing
a lot of energy

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:47.290
in making ML benchmarks
reproducible via open source

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:48.350
implementations.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:50.480
So you don't have to
trust my word for it.

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:51.940
You can take
anything that you're

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:54.670
about to see, run it
yourselves on Cloud TPUs,

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:58.530
and see if you measure
the same results.

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:00.950
The best and most
carefully designed

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:03.920
contest we've seen so far has
been organized by some Stanford

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:04.640
researchers.

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.080
It's called DAWNBench.

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:08.240
And they really focused
on these metrics

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.940
that I just mentioned,
time-to-accuracy

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:14.540
and total cost, especially
on ImageNet, but also

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:18.440
on CIFAR-10 and
question-answering dataset.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:21.380
And so I'm happy to report
that Cloud TPUs came

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:25.730
in number one in DAWNBench
for ImageNet training cost,

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:29.870
hitting their accuracy in
just 7.5 hours for under $50.

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:32.000
But now, the field
is moving so fast,

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.850
this was earlier
this year, already

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:36.680
with the new
promotional pricing,

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.630
that comes down to $34.

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:42.230
And with preemptibles,
that's just over $10,

00:29:42.230 --> 00:29:45.410
which is kind of amazing.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:47.420
But wait, there's more.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:50.600
If we look at the ResNet
results here, which at that time

00:29:50.600 --> 00:29:55.166
were achieving something
like 3,250 images a second,

00:29:55.166 --> 00:29:56.540
and hitting the
accuracy that you

00:29:56.540 --> 00:30:01.190
expect in just under nine
hours, that cost just under $60.

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:04.010
Now that's down to $40,
running the exact same code,

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:08.720
or just under $13 at
preemptible prices.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.370
The pod, as I promised,
scales up linearly.

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:13.700
So a half-pod was able
to get the job done, not

00:30:13.700 --> 00:30:16.314
in hours, but in 30 minutes.

00:30:16.314 --> 00:30:18.230
And, actually, that
includes a lot of overhead

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:21.150
that doesn't really make a
lot of sense at this scale,

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:24.027
because you end up having to
checkpoint every few seconds

00:30:24.027 --> 00:30:26.360
as you're getting through the
DAWNBench challenge, which

00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:27.568
you wouldn't do in real life.

00:30:27.568 --> 00:30:29.420
So if you eliminate the
checkpoint overhead,

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:33.114
that's just 23.9 minutes to
get to this level of accuracy.

00:30:33.114 --> 00:30:35.030
That's really what I
mean about enabling teams

00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:37.578
to iterate faster than
they ever could before.

00:30:37.578 --> 00:30:39.929
SPEAKER 2: How
much did that cost?

00:30:39.929 --> 00:30:40.970
SPEAKER 1: Good question.

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:42.760
Talk to Sales.

00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:46.030
So pricing is not
announced for alpha,

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:48.820
but I highly encourage you
to talk to our sales team

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:52.600
to get a sense of
what this will cost.

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:57.510
Furthermore, I'd like to
say that at DAWNBench there

00:30:57.510 --> 00:30:59.480
was a third-place entry
that was very clever.

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:01.230
It was from a team
called fast.ai.

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:04.820
And they had an entry
at $72 at the time

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:06.120
that had two clever tricks.

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:08.694
One was progressively
scaling up the image size

00:31:08.694 --> 00:31:10.110
during training,
because you don't

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:11.680
need the higher resolution
at the very beginning,

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:12.222
it turns out.

00:31:12.222 --> 00:31:14.305
And they also used a more
aggressive learning rate

00:31:14.305 --> 00:31:15.160
schedule.

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:17.850
And so after the
contest, we reproduced

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.800
these algorithmic
changes on the Cloud TPU,

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:22.560
and open sourced the
changes, and that

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.560
takes ResNet-50 training
costs on Cloud TPU

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:29.400
from the new $40.83 down to $17.

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:32.670
And that is affordable even with
predictable on-demand pricing.

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:34.470
But with preemptible
pricing, the cost

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:36.570
is now down to just $5.

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:39.240
So we're obviously trying to
drive this as close to zero

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:41.940
as we can, because
we know that you

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.220
need to train these
models over and over again

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:45.150
to make progress.

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:46.608
And you want to
train a lot of them

00:31:46.608 --> 00:31:48.750
in parallel to do a gigantic
hyperparameter search

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:51.450
or neural architecture
search on every new product

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:53.200
that you're going to release.

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:56.780
So to experience
this performance,

00:31:56.780 --> 00:31:58.410
like I mentioned
before, Cloud TPUs

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:00.290
are now GA and widely available.

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:03.410
Start with our Quickstart
or our tutorials.

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.180
Use one of our reference models.

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:09.610
And, hopefully, this
will be helpful to you.

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:11.270
So where do we go
from DAWNBench?

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:14.110
Well, there a new benchmark
conference in town

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:16.470
that's even more
comprehensive and challenging

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:19.320
that's organized across
many different institutions,

00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:20.980
companies, universities.

00:32:20.980 --> 00:32:23.550
And you can find more
information at MLPerf,

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:26.460
mlperf.org.

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:28.980
I think this is going to be
really exciting with results

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:33.290
for our version 0.5 of the
benchmark late this October.

00:32:33.290 --> 00:32:37.430
So how do you start using these
Cloud TPUs, or TPU pods, today?

00:32:37.430 --> 00:32:40.790
Well, I recommend that you
start with one of our reference

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:41.630
models.

00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:43.130
And I want to
emphasize that we have

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:45.290
a wide range of these
reference models

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:46.910
that are great starting points.

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:50.030
Even if you're heavily invested
in some model in-house,

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:52.310
it's useful to step back
and say, well, wait.

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:54.290
What is the
underlying task here?

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:57.830
Whether it's object detection or
speech recognition or language

00:32:57.830 --> 00:32:59.540
processing or
something else, and see

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:03.020
if we have a reference model
that corresponds to that task.

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.400
Because then, just
as a baseline,

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:07.580
you can take your data, connect
it to our reference model--

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:10.070
and here's a whole range
of our reference models--

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:12.900
and immediately get a
result in just a few days,

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:14.930
maybe even an
afternoon, before you

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:18.290
think about the more intensive
process of taking your existing

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:22.010
model, and porting it over to a
new system, and optimizing it.

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:25.490
So as you can see here, we've
got image recognition, object

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:28.580
detection, machine translation,
language modeling, speech

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:30.320
recognition, and even
image generation,

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:32.870
some of the cutting-edge
stuff, over on the right,

00:33:32.870 --> 00:33:34.980
and we're adding new
models all the time.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:36.830
So I'll highlight
a few of these,

00:33:36.830 --> 00:33:38.720
and provide some new
performance numbers,

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:42.170
and sort of a mini-case
study on object detection.

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:44.900
So you've heard a lot
about image classification.

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:48.050
I'd like to just call out that
our AmoebaNet-D model that

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:51.470
won DAWNBench is actually
an architecture that

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:56.010
was discovered through neural
architecture search on the TPU.

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:58.460
So it's a model that was
designed from scratch

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:01.980
on the TPU, made available
open source for all of you,

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:04.790
and it achieves fantastic
accuracy on ImageNet, and also

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:05.800
on larger data sets.

00:34:08.545 --> 00:34:10.170
Object detection is
really interesting,

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:12.210
because here you're
processing larger images

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:15.929
and trying to localize many
different objects within them.

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:19.590
And so, back in Google I/O
in May, I showed this result,

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:21.090
but I've updated
the prices here for

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:22.909
our new promotional pricing.

00:34:22.909 --> 00:34:25.530
You're processing
896 by 896 images.

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:28.199
And at that time, we
achieve 37 average precision

00:34:28.199 --> 00:34:31.090
on COCO, which is pretty
good, in just six hours.

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:33.540
And now that would cost
you only $28, or just $9

00:34:33.540 --> 00:34:34.889
with preemptibles.

00:34:34.889 --> 00:34:39.900
But with TensorFlow 1.9, we've
made even greater advances

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:41.455
in efficiency.

00:34:41.455 --> 00:34:43.080
And that means if
you're willing to let

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:46.824
this run just a little bit
longer, you can get to 37.7 AP

00:34:46.824 --> 00:34:47.460
on COCO.

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:48.630
So that's up from 37.

00:34:48.630 --> 00:34:49.590
That's pretty cool.

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:52.050
It's not that much
more expensive.

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:55.800
But now, this is just
without any code changes

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:58.980
going from TPU v2 to TPU
v3, because, remember,

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:00.420
this is a family of processors.

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:02.670
We want to make it
as easy as possible

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:04.920
for you to scale up
from, whether it's

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:08.320
one device up to a pod, or from
one generation to the next.

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:11.070
So here, RetineNet
with TensorFlow 1.9

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:14.310
can achieve that same
37.7 average position

00:35:14.310 --> 00:35:16.420
on COCO in just 4.1 hours.

00:35:16.420 --> 00:35:19.057
So it's 1.5x faster
with no code changes.

00:35:19.057 --> 00:35:21.390
And we've barely begun to
optimize for the new platform.

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:26.354
What's even more
interesting than that

00:35:26.354 --> 00:35:28.520
is what happens when you
have this additional memory

00:35:28.520 --> 00:35:29.120
available.

00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:29.620
Right?

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:31.940
Because the memory
doubles for v2 to v3.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:34.400
So, suddenly, without a
lot of engineering effort,

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:36.986
you can process 1024
by 1024 images, which

00:35:36.986 --> 00:35:38.360
I know is something
that's really

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:40.110
interesting to a lot
of autonomous driving

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:42.710
companies or medical imaging
companies that want to process

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:44.630
the largest images they can.

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:47.580
Suddenly, you're up to
38.4 average precision.

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:51.230
Remember, up from 37
originally, in just 5.2 hours.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.780
And now that you're able
to iterate at this larger

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:56.960
scale and larger speed, you
try out some data augmentation

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:59.690
techniques, and that gets
you to 41 average precision

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.160
in just 10 hours, letting
it run a little longer.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.070
So this is a great
concrete example

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:06.110
of the general principle
I introduced before--

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:09.710
the faster hardware, and also
larger memory in this case,

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:12.482
gives you the opportunity for
more experimentation and even

00:36:12.482 --> 00:36:13.940
higher accuracy
for the models that

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:16.541
matter most to your business.

00:36:16.541 --> 00:36:18.540
Furthermore, I'm thrilled
to announce that image

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.320
segmentation is coming soon.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:22.490
So we've got the
DeepLab v3 model coming.

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:24.970
Mask R-CNN, which I
know is a popular model

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:27.575
out there, and more, for
those of you who don't just

00:36:27.575 --> 00:36:28.950
want bounding
boxes, but you want

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:33.470
to know pixel by pixel where
each object is in an image.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:36.050
I'll just emphasize
that TPUs are not

00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:38.460
only about computer vision.

00:36:38.460 --> 00:36:41.690
Here's machine translation with
transformer achieving a near

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:45.930
state-of-the-art blue score on
WMT 14 from English to German,

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:49.730
6.2 hours, under $30, under
$10 for the preemptible.

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:50.960
It's fantastic.

00:36:50.960 --> 00:36:54.710
Language modeling, it's more
compute-intensive to train

00:36:54.710 --> 00:36:57.584
on the language model
one-billion-word dataset.

00:36:57.584 --> 00:36:59.000
But this is the
kind of thing that

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:02.060
lets you do things like Smart
Reply or Smart Compose, that's

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:03.890
helpful for messaging
apps and any kind

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:06.800
of conversational interaction
where you want to save people

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:08.570
time predicting what's next.

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:10.550
And this is an example
of one of several models

00:37:10.550 --> 00:37:12.350
that's in Tensor2Tensor.

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:14.270
There's a link down
here at the bottom.

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:16.680
Two is a numeral, and
it's all one word.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:19.547
And Tensor2Tensor is built
on top of TensorFlow.

00:37:19.547 --> 00:37:21.380
It's great for research,
mixing and matching

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:24.530
data sets, small architectures,
training processes.

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:27.025
And you'll see a few others
like this one also there.

00:37:27.025 --> 00:37:28.400
So speech recognition
here, we've

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:32.720
got ASR Transformer giving you
a great word error rate in just

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:35.750
13 hours, 7.9 word error rate.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:37.610
And you can get down
to 7.3, if you're

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:39.360
willing to let it
train a little longer.

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:43.090
And, again, preemptible
pricing, just under 20 bucks.

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:46.679
Finally-- well, almost
finally-- question answering.

00:37:46.679 --> 00:37:48.970
This was the winner of the
DAWNBench Question Answering

00:37:48.970 --> 00:37:49.810
Contest.

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:52.279
And the team has continued
to make improvements such

00:37:52.279 --> 00:37:54.070
that this now trains
in just eight minutes.

00:37:54.070 --> 00:37:55.930
I've written estimated
training costs here,

00:37:55.930 --> 00:37:57.550
because this is
now so fast we may

00:37:57.550 --> 00:37:59.980
have to rethink the other
overheads to see how to measure

00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:02.160
this cost accurately.

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:04.720
It's too small to even register.

00:38:04.720 --> 00:38:06.430
And that question
answering model

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:08.950
now has an open source
implementation available,

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:10.360
which is great.

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:14.410
So also at the cutting edge
of research image generation,

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:16.810
bits per dimension is
the key metric here.

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:19.070
And lower is actually better.

00:38:19.070 --> 00:38:22.450
So this 2.98 is close to the
2.92 that's state of the art.

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:26.800
For just $130, or $44
with preemptible pricing,

00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:29.440
you can generate these images
from scratch that look weirdly

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.240
like real life, but not quite.

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:34.480
So I think this is a really
interesting research domain.

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:38.560
So please consider starting
with a reference model.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:41.400
Even if you love the model
that you've already built,

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:43.900
even if you've sunk an enormous
amount of engineering effort

00:38:43.900 --> 00:38:48.040
into it, it's really valuable to
just lay down a quick baseline

00:38:48.040 --> 00:38:51.040
with a public dataset, or with
one of your in-house datasets,

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:54.280
using these reference models
to then guide your engineering

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:56.920
choices, whether it's
porting your existing model,

00:38:56.920 --> 00:38:59.410
maybe merging it with some
of our reference code,

00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:01.670
or starting with a
reference model fresh,

00:39:01.670 --> 00:39:04.100
and then building from there.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.097
So these reference models
are high performance.

00:39:06.097 --> 00:39:06.930
They're open source.

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:08.105
They're cutting-edge.

00:39:08.105 --> 00:39:10.229
You know, some of the latest
and greatest research,

00:39:10.229 --> 00:39:11.810
like AmoebaNet, is
immediately pushed

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:15.020
into this pool of reference
models that are open source.

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:17.330
We're doing a lot of continuous
testing for performance

00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:20.340
and accuracy to save you as
much of that trouble as we can,

00:39:20.340 --> 00:39:22.577
because we know it's
complicated and expensive.

00:39:22.577 --> 00:39:25.160
And these reference models let
you get up and running quickly,

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:27.270
training on your own data.

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:29.520
But I want to
emphasize you're not

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:32.220
limited to reference models.

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:35.570
For example, here's another case
study of architecture research

00:39:35.570 --> 00:39:39.420
that a group at Stanford
did where they actually

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:43.200
did parallel runs using hundreds
of Cloud TPUs simultaneously.

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:50.310
Each blue dot in this image is
a run training an ImageNet scale

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:52.950
Convolutional RNN on a TPU.

00:39:52.950 --> 00:39:55.025
So this is a lot of
computation here.

00:39:55.025 --> 00:39:56.400
They were searching
for a model--

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:58.165
there's an archive
link in lower right--

00:39:58.165 --> 00:40:00.540
"Task-Driven Convolutional
Recurrent Models of the Visual

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:02.197
System" is the paper--

00:40:02.197 --> 00:40:03.780
and they were looking
for a model that

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:05.910
was a better fit for
the types of dynamics

00:40:05.910 --> 00:40:09.720
that you record if you put
electrodes in the brain

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:12.600
and actually listen to
the primate visual cortex

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:15.060
as it's seeing.

00:40:15.060 --> 00:40:18.020
And so, here's a diagram
of the sort of space

00:40:18.020 --> 00:40:19.310
that they were searching.

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:21.770
And it turns out that
across this population

00:40:21.770 --> 00:40:23.387
of many different
models, they found

00:40:23.387 --> 00:40:25.970
that these red connections were
selected for during the search

00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:27.410
versus the others.

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:29.360
And then when they
went back and tried

00:40:29.360 --> 00:40:31.640
to fit these models
to some of the signals

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:34.670
that they were actually
recording in primates,

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:37.390
they found that these
Convolutional RNNs

00:40:37.390 --> 00:40:41.390
were a much better fit for
the neural signals in v4,

00:40:41.390 --> 00:40:46.190
in IT, than typical just
ordinary convolutional

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:49.310
feed-forward models
that you see typically

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:50.450
in the literature today.

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:52.430
So I think this is an
interesting new research

00:40:52.430 --> 00:40:53.151
direction.

00:40:53.151 --> 00:40:55.400
And it's a group that was
able to do this from scratch

00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:58.650
on their own, using
hundreds of Cloud TPUs.

00:40:58.650 --> 00:41:01.530
So you can not only
implement your own models,

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:05.180
you can search for models
automatically using Cloud TPUs.

00:41:05.180 --> 00:41:07.646
So I want to just share a
little bit more information

00:41:07.646 --> 00:41:09.020
about the hardware,
just give you

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:10.850
a flavor of what makes
these accelerators

00:41:10.850 --> 00:41:13.790
different from other
types of processors that

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:15.800
are available on the market.

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:18.530
So the Cloud TPU
that I mentioned

00:41:18.530 --> 00:41:21.950
before consists of this device
here, which under those heat

00:41:21.950 --> 00:41:26.210
sinks has four separate
processors and all of this 64

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:28.830
gigabytes of high bandwidth
memory with a very high memory

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:31.670
bandwidth among the processors.

00:41:31.670 --> 00:41:34.580
And it's connected
to a host server,

00:41:34.580 --> 00:41:37.400
like you see here, with PCI v3.

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:41.120
And so let's zoom in on just one
of these chips in the Cloud TPU

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:42.200
device.

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:44.430
Well, that chip has
a layout like this.

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:46.520
It's got two cores.

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:48.740
And it's got a scaler unit.

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:50.030
It's got a vector unit.

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:52.970
And most importantly,
it's got this matrix unit,

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:55.217
which is this gigantic
systolic array.

00:41:55.217 --> 00:41:57.050
That's an old idea in
hardware architecture,

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.000
but it's newly relevant
now that so many

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:01.640
of these cutting-edge models
use dense linear algebra.

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:05.370
It's really the perfect fit
for this systolic array.

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:07.830
One interesting thing
about this systolic array

00:42:07.830 --> 00:42:10.410
is that while it's doing
float32 accumulate, like you're

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:13.290
used to, it's actually using
a different filling point

00:42:13.290 --> 00:42:16.560
format behind the scenes with no
code changes for you necessary,

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:19.110
although you can take
further advantage of bfloat16

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:20.710
if you want.

00:42:20.710 --> 00:42:22.950
So it's using bfloat16
and it multiplies.

00:42:22.950 --> 00:42:25.485
And let me tell you a little
bit more about what that means,

00:42:25.485 --> 00:42:27.651
because I think this is one
of the unique advantages

00:42:27.651 --> 00:42:28.930
of the Cloud TPU.

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:34.500
So float32 IEEE floating
point has this enormous range.

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:36.930
And those 32 bits are
divided as you see here

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:39.390
with the exponent and
then the mantissa.

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:42.210
Lots of bits for the mantissa
to track these fine differences

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:43.320
between numbers.

00:42:43.320 --> 00:42:45.840
That's important for high
performance computing

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:48.079
where you're tracking fine
details in a simulation,

00:42:48.079 --> 00:42:50.370
but it turns out it doesn't
matter that much in machine

00:42:50.370 --> 00:42:51.780
learning.

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:55.940
So some vendors have
gone to IEEE float16,

00:42:55.940 --> 00:42:58.820
which you can see here,
which reduces both

00:42:58.820 --> 00:43:00.710
the exponent and the mantissa.

00:43:00.710 --> 00:43:03.510
And what that means is you
end up reducing the range.

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:05.450
And now if you're
programming with float16,

00:43:05.450 --> 00:43:08.510
you have to completely rewrite
your model in a different way

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:10.340
on a model-by-model basis.

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:12.530
It's very time-consuming
and subtle,

00:43:12.530 --> 00:43:15.020
because you have to keep
all of your activations

00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:16.280
in the right ranges.

00:43:16.280 --> 00:43:17.781
You might have to
do loss rescaling.

00:43:17.781 --> 00:43:20.113
We're trying in TensorFlow
to make this more convenient,

00:43:20.113 --> 00:43:21.180
but it's really hard.

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:24.490
And so we actually use a
different floating point format

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:27.750
in the TPU that we
call the bfloat16,

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:30.630
for the brain team, which
is where we started this.

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:33.110
And there we preserve
the apex of exponents,

00:43:33.110 --> 00:43:34.670
and just chop off the mantissa.

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:36.560
Turns out, this is also
a drop in replacement

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:39.230
for float32 across this huge
range of machine learning

00:43:39.230 --> 00:43:40.700
models that we see.

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:43.910
You only have to be careful in
a few small situations-- maybe

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:46.040
atom has a parameter
that you've got

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:48.567
to be careful to
explicitly cast a float32,

00:43:48.567 --> 00:43:50.900
because there are so many
nines after the decimal point.

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:54.860
But in general, it's much
easier to work with bfloat16

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:56.210
than with float32.

00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:58.490
And then that reduces
your memory usage

00:43:58.490 --> 00:44:00.610
and avoids memory bottlenecks.

00:44:00.610 --> 00:44:02.680
It's fantastic.

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:05.002
And if you want to
see how bloat16 works,

00:44:05.002 --> 00:44:06.710
a lot of our reference
models are already

00:44:06.710 --> 00:44:08.010
using it transparently.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:11.070
So you can see some
examples in, you know,

00:44:11.070 --> 00:44:14.120
the GitHub TensorFlow
TPU repository.

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:17.870
So just to illustrate briefly
how this matrix unit actually

00:44:17.870 --> 00:44:20.060
does magic, here's
a 3 by 3 example,

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:23.030
but imagine this is 128 by
128, which is what we really

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:24.900
have on the device.

00:44:24.900 --> 00:44:27.410
So what's happening as
the data's streaming in

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:30.020
is we're getting a
lot of great reuse

00:44:30.020 --> 00:44:33.290
of these intermediate results
within the systolic array

00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:36.440
in this staggered fashion that's
producing the output that we

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:37.050
want.

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:41.180
And so as long as your problem
has some dense linear algebra

00:44:41.180 --> 00:44:44.660
core that can be
mapped efficiently

00:44:44.660 --> 00:44:47.000
to these systolic
arrays, which we found

00:44:47.000 --> 00:44:49.250
is possible across this
wide range of applications

00:44:49.250 --> 00:44:51.440
that I called out
earlier, this is

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:56.750
going to be a great fit for your
machine learning, both training

00:44:56.750 --> 00:44:59.440
and also inference.

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:01.200
So stepping back
for just a second.

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:02.580
We've covered a lot.

00:45:02.580 --> 00:45:05.930
I just want to
emphasize Cloud TPU v2.

00:45:05.930 --> 00:45:08.160
It's now GA, it's
widely available.

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:09.390
It's in the US.

00:45:09.390 --> 00:45:10.260
It's in Asia.

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:11.440
It's in Europe.

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:12.600
We've got regular pricing.

00:45:12.600 --> 00:45:14.280
We've got preemptible pricing.

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:16.674
It's an incredibly affordable
way to start with one

00:45:16.674 --> 00:45:18.840
of our state-of-the-art
reference models and explore

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:20.460
this frontier if
you haven't yet.

00:45:20.460 --> 00:45:22.770
Or if you're one
of these folks part

00:45:22.770 --> 00:45:25.650
of or leading a team of these
cutting-edge machine learning

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:27.120
engineers and
researchers, they'll

00:45:27.120 --> 00:45:29.250
thank you for having access
to orders of magnitude

00:45:29.250 --> 00:45:33.160
more compute than you could
possibly have afforded before.

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:36.510
We've also gone through how to
measure performance carefully

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:38.595
and looked at some
of these applications

00:45:38.595 --> 00:45:40.470
across all these different
domains-- not just

00:45:40.470 --> 00:45:43.680
image recognition, but also
speech, language, image

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:44.850
generation.

00:45:44.850 --> 00:45:47.020
And one other thing I'd
like to highlight here

00:45:47.020 --> 00:45:48.425
is that Cloud TPUs--

00:45:48.425 --> 00:45:53.280
I focused on using Cloud TPUs
in Google Compute Engine,

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.850
but that's just the lowest
level of the stack, where

00:45:55.850 --> 00:45:58.500
we're giving you maximum
flexibility and control.

00:45:58.500 --> 00:46:00.660
We're also working to
integrate Cloud TPUs with all

00:46:00.660 --> 00:46:02.160
of the rest of Google Cloud.

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:04.380
On the storage side, which
I haven't written here,

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:07.650
with GCS, but also with
Bigtable, and with Arbitrade,

00:46:07.650 --> 00:46:12.602
gRPC services, and then
here with Cloud ML engine,

00:46:12.602 --> 00:46:14.310
if you want to use a
higher-level managed

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:16.059
service to keep track
of all your training

00:46:16.059 --> 00:46:19.440
jobs, and Kubernetes
GKE, there's

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:22.710
a really interesting
talk tomorrow on Minigo,

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:27.720
training an AlphaGo
Zero-like model on hundreds

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:29.574
of Cloud TPUs using GKE.

00:46:29.574 --> 00:46:30.990
It's going to be
a fantastic talk,

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:33.550
so check it out, and look
for the recording afterwards.

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:37.560
So we're really
introducing a new platform

00:46:37.560 --> 00:46:39.480
for machine learning.

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:41.940
It works well with TensorFlow,
but also with Keras.

00:46:41.940 --> 00:46:44.040
And we're looking at
other frameworks as well.

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:45.930
And we hope it'll
be valuable to you,

00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:48.030
as you're developing
cutting-edge new

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:51.985
machine-learning-enabled
applications for your customers

00:46:51.985 --> 00:46:52.860
all around the world.

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:54.330
So thanks very much.

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:57.380
